      en: 'The Varendra Research Museum is the oldest museum in Bangladesh, founded in 1910 through the efforts of the Varendra Research Society. Sarat Kumar Ray of the Dighapatia royal family, together with historians Akshay Kumar Maitreya and Ramaprasad Chanda, toured the villages of North Bengal collecting antiquities that became the core of the collection. Today it is run by Rajshahi University.\n\nInside the handsome red building, galleries hold black-stone sculptures of the Pala and Sena periods — Vishnu, Surya, the Buddha and many goddesses — along with ancient coins, inscriptions, a few objects from Mohenjo-daro, Sultanate and Mughal weapons, and old hand-written manuscripts. Many pieces come from Paharpur and other sites of the Varendra region.\n\nThe halls are quiet and cool, so it is a good stop even on a hot afternoon. If you plan to visit Paharpur or Mahasthangarh, an hour here first will give the ruins far more meaning.'
