      en: 'Thirteen or so kilometres north of Bogura town, on the bank of the Karatoya, lie the ruins of Pundranagara, today called Mahasthangarh. A Brahmi inscription found here is generally dated to the 3rd century BCE, making it the oldest known urban site in Bangladesh. People lived here continuously for well over a thousand years, through the Maurya, Gupta and Pala periods and into the Sultanate era.\n\nYou can walk along the high earthen ramparts of the roughly rectangular citadel, and scattered around it are numerous excavated mounds such as Bairagir Bhita, Khodar Pathar Bhita, Mankalir Kunda and Govinda Bhita. In one corner of the citadel is the shrine of the Sufi saint Shah Sultan Balkhi Mahisawar, busy with pilgrims all year. The site museum opposite Govinda Bhita holds coins, sculpture, terracotta plaques and ornaments from the excavations.\n\nIn the cool season, walking the ramparts above green rice fields and the slow Karatoya makes this at least a half-day destination for anyone interested in history.'
