      en: 'In Jagaddal village, Dhamoirhat upazila, close to the Indian border, lie the ruins of Jagaddala Mahavihara, a renowned Buddhist centre of learning of the Pala period. Tibetan sources suggest it was founded by the Pala king Ramapala in the late 11th century. Scholars here wrote and translated Buddhist texts, some of which survive in Tibetan collections.\n\nExcavations since the 1990s have revealed rows of monks’ cells, brick walls, terracotta plaques and some sculpture. The site is far smaller than Paharpur and sees very few visitors, which gives a walk among the ruins in the open fields a rare, intimate stillness.\n\nComing here on a winter morning, through green fields and silent villages, feels like walking back in time. Altadighi National Park is nearby, so the two make a natural pair.'
